ation'. It also supports the 3-option scope (well within the ~5 source limit).\n\nPattern guidance (seed `:summary`):\nComparative summary is the multi-source research-then-synthesize variant: parallel per-source extraction → :code aggregator structures extractions per source → final :llm synthesizes a structured comparison (common / differences / recommendation). Always use :parallel (not :sequence) for independent per-source stages — wall-time becomes max(per-source). CRITICAL: use IDENTICAL :output-schemas across per-source stages so comparable fields stay aligned. Limit to ~5 sources per synthesis; for more, do hierarchical comparison. Use contract-comparison (parallel-independent task-class) when the structure is exactly diff-two-versions; comparative-summary is for general N-source comparison.\n\nCapabilities:\n  - :sequence of :parallel (or :map-each over sources) per-source :llm extraction → :code aggregator → :llm synthesizer that produces a structured comparison\n  - natural for any 'read N versions/sources and produce a comparison' work — product comparison, contract diff, multi-source fact verification\n  - outputs are typically a structured comparison object (per-source key, common-elements, differences, recommendation)\n\nStrengths (proven traits — these patterns have been observed to work; mimic where they fit, adapt as needed):\n  - **Trait:** the synthesis stage's :reads include ALL per-source extractions structured as a per-source map — supports apples-to-apples comparison by source rather than free-form prose (confidence 1.00, evidence-count 1)\n    - Good when: the sources are roughly comparable AND a structured comparison is the value (vs free-form narrative)\n    - Worked example DSL (corpus reference — adapt to your task):\n      ```clojure\n      [:sequence [:parallel [:llm {:reads [:source-a] :writes [:extract-a]}] [:llm {:reads [:source-b] :writes [:extract-b]}]] [:code {:reads [:extract-a :extract-b] :writes [:by-source] :fn (fn [{:keys [extract-a extract-b]}] {:by-source {:a extract-a :b extract-b}})}] [:llm {:reads [:by-source] :writes [:common :differences :recommendation]}] [:final {:keys [:common :differences :recommendation]}]]\n      ```\n\n  - **Trait:** use :parallel (NOT :sequence) for the per-source extraction stages when sources are independent — extraction wall-time becomes max(per-source) not sum(per-source) (confidence 1.00, evidence-count 1)\n    - Good when: sources are independent (no need to read source B before extracting source A)\n    - Worked example DSL (corpus reference — adapt to your task):\n      ```clojure\n      [:parallel [:llm {:reads [:source-a] :writes [:extract-a]}] [:llm {:reads [:source-b] :writes [:extract-b]}]]\n      ```\n\nWeaknesses (observed failure modes — avoid these patterns, apply the recommended fix where applicable):\n  - **Failure mode:** free-form per-source extraction (different :output-schemas per source or open-ended free text) makes the synthesizer's apples-to-apples comparison hard — comparable fields drift across sources (confidence 1.00, evidence-count 1)\n    - Avoid when: per-source extractions use different schemas OR free-form text\n    - Recommended fix: always use IDENTICAL :output-schemas across per-source extraction stages — comparable fields stay aligned, synthesizer can do direct per-field diff\n\n  - **Failure mode:** synthesizing more than ~5 sources in a single :llm call exceeds context — the comparison loses fidelity on per-source detail (confidence 1.00, evidence-count 1)\n    - Avoid when: source count >5 AND each source's extraction is non-trivial\n    - Recommended fix: for >5 sources, hierarchical comparison: cluster sources into 2-3 groups → comparative-summary within each group → final cross-group comparison\n\nRepresentative uses (concrete tasks this pattern has shipped on):\n  - product-feature comparison across 3-4 vendor docs: per-vendor extraction → side-by-side feature matrix\n  - contract diff across 2 versions: per-version extraction → key-differences + similarities + recommendation\n  - multi-source fact verification: per-source claim extraction → common claims + contradictions + confidence\n\n#### Alternative #1 — Comparative summary (confidence: 0.95)\nWhy this fits: This fingerprint matches the comparative-summary class perfectly, providing the necessary parallel extraction and synthesis structure for the three database options. It is ranked slightly lower than the class itself as the class provides the broader definition for this multi-source synthesis task.\n\nPattern guidance (seed `:summary`):\nComparative summary is the multi-source research-then-synthesize variant: parallel per-source extraction → :code aggregator structures extractions per source → final :llm synthesizes a structured comparison (common / differences / recommendation). Always use :parallel (not :sequence) for independent per-source stages — wall-time becomes max(per-source). CRITICAL: use IDENTICAL :output-schemas across per-source stages so comparable fields stay aligned. Limit to ~5 sources per synthesis; for more, do hierarchical comparison. Use contract-comparison (parallel-independent task-class) when the structure is exactly diff-two-versions; comparative-summary is for general N-source comparison.\n\nCapabilities:\n  - :sequence of :parallel (or :map-each over sources) per-source :llm extraction → :code aggregator → :llm synthesizer that produces a structured comparison\n  - natural for any 'read N versions/sources and produce a comparison' work — product comparison, contract diff, multi-source fact verification\n  - outputs are typically a structured comparison object (per-source key, common-elements, differences, recommendation)\n\nStrengths (proven traits — these patterns have been observed to work; mimic where they fit, adapt as needed):\n  - **Trait:** the synthesis stage's :reads include ALL per-source extractions structured as a per-source map — supports apples-to-apples comparison by source rather than free-form prose (confidence 1.00, evidence-count 1)\n    - Good when: the sources are roughly comparable AND a structured comparison is the value (vs free-form narrative)\n    - Worked example DSL (corpus reference — adapt to your task):\n      ```clojure\n      [:sequence [:parallel [:llm {:reads [:source-a] :writes [:extract-a]}] [:llm {:reads [:source-b] :writes [:extract-b]}]] [:code {:reads [:extract-a :extract-b] :writes [:by-source] :fn (fn [{:keys [extract-a extract-b]}] {:by-source {:a extract-a :b extract-b}})}] [:llm {:reads [:by-source] :writes [:common :differences :recommendation]}] [:final {:keys [:common :differences :recommendation]}]]\n      ```\n\n  - **Trait:** use :parallel (NOT :sequence) for the per-source extraction stages when sources are independent — extraction wall-time becomes max(per-source) not sum(per-source) (confidence 1.00, evidence-count 1)\n    - Good when: sources are independent (no need to read source B before extracting source A)\n    - Worked example DSL (corpus reference — adapt to your task):\n      ```clojure\n      [:parallel [:llm {:reads [:source-a] :writes [:extract-a]}] [:llm {:reads [:source-b] :writes [:extract-b]}]]\n      ```\n\nWeaknesses (observed failure modes — avoid these patterns, apply the recommended fix where applicable):\n  - **Failure mode:** free-form per-source extraction (different :output-schemas per source or open-ended free text) makes the synthesizer's apples-to-apples comparison hard — comparable fields drift across sources (confidence 1.00, evidence-count 1)\n    - Avoid when: per-source extractions use different schemas OR free-form text\n    - Recommended fix: always use IDENTICAL :output-schemas across per-source extraction stages — comparable fields stay aligned, synthesizer can do direct per-field diff\n\n  - **Failure mode:** synthesizing more than ~5 sources in a single :llm call exceeds context — the comparison loses fidelity on per-source detail (confidence 1.00, evidence-count 1)\n    - Avoid when: source count >5 AND each source's extraction is non-trivial\n    - Recommended fix: for >5 sources, hierarchical comparison: cluster sources into 2-3 groups → comparative-summary within each group → final cross-group comparison\n\nRepresentative uses (concrete tasks this pattern has shipped on):\n  - product-feature comparison across 3-4 vendor docs: per-vendor extraction → side-by-side feature matrix\n  - contract diff across 2 versions: per-version extraction → key-differences + similarities + recommendation\n  - multi-source fact verification: per-source claim extraction → common claims + contradictions + confidence\n\n### Behavioral competencies (top 3 from corpus retrieval)\n1. Behavioral: Synthesis (confidence: 0.95)\n   Why this fits: The task requires integrating three structured options (Postgres, Vector DB, Hybrid) and multiple constraints (team size, expertise, timeline) into a cohesive recommendation. The 'Synthesis' subtree's recommended-pattern for long artifacts (building a :code-built outline to preserve coverage) directly maps to the required output sections like :option-summaries and :fit-to-team-constraints. Its focus on 'attribution' and 'balancing per-source weight' ensures the recommendation is grounded in the specific team context provided in the query.\n\n   Guidance (seed `:summary`):\n   Synthesis integrates multiple structured inputs into a single cohesive artifact (narrative / report / recommendation). Always provide the synthesizer with inputs structured by source-id (not free-form concat) so attribution stays intact. For long artifacts, scaffold via a :code-built outline that the :llm fills section-by-section — preserves coverage. NEVER synthesize >5 substantive inputs in one call: do hierarchical clustering instead. Declare :output-schemas on the synthesis output when downstream stages (critique / validation) need to verify specific sections. Composes into briefing-generation, comparative-summary, and sequential-pipeline shells. Avoid when the task is ideation (new options) or critique (checking against criteria).\n\nCapabilities:\n  - given multiple structured inputs (findings, extracts, source quotes), produce a single integrated artifact — narrative, report, recommendation\n  - natural fit at the tail of a tree where multiple parallel branches converge\n  - outputs are typically a single string (or small structured map) where the structure of the inputs has been collapsed into coherent prose\n\nStrengths (proven traits — these patterns have been observed to work; mimic where they fit, adapt as needed):\n  - **Trait:** make the synthesizer's :reads contain the per-source extractions structured by source-id rather than a free-form concat — supports apples-to-apples reference and avoids the model losing track of which finding came from where (confidence 1.00, evidence-count 1)\n    - Good when: synthesis needs to attribute claims to sources OR balance per-source weight\n    - Worked example DSL (corpus reference — adapt to your task):\n      ```clojure\n      [:llm {:reads [:by-source] :writes [:narrative] :output-schemas {:narrative :string}}]\n      ```\n\n  - **Trait:** for long final artifacts, scaffold the synthesis with a :code node that pre-builds a structured outline from the inputs, then have the :llm fill each section — preserves coverage of all inputs (confidence 1.00, evidence-count 1)\n    - Good when: the final artifact is multi-section AND each section maps to a distinct cluster of inputs\n    - Worked example DSL (corpus reference — adapt to your task):\n      ```clojure\n      [:sequence [:code {:reads [:findings] :writes [:outline] :fn (fn [{:keys [inputs]}] {:outline (mapv :type (:findings inputs))})}] [:llm {:reads [:findings :outline] :writes [:narrative]}]]\n      ```\n\nWeaknesses (observed failure modes — avoid these patterns, apply the recommended fix where applicable):\n  - **Failure mode:** synthesizing >5 distinct inputs in a single :llm call exceeds context — the model drops inputs from the middle of the context window silently (confidence 1.00, evidence-count 1)\n    - Avoid when: input count >5 AND each input is substantive\n    - Recommended fix: hierarchical synthesis: cluster inputs into 2-3 groups → per-group :llm synthesis → final :llm integrates the group narratives\n\n  - **Failure mode:** free-form synthesis output (no :output-schemas) makes downstream stages (critique / validation) work on unstructured prose — they can't easily check coverage or shape (confidence 1.00, evidence-count 1)\n    - Avoid when: downstream stages need to verify specific sections are present\n    - Recommended fix: declare a structured :output-schemas on the synthesis (e.g., :map with :summary :recommendations :open-questions) — downstream critique/validation can verify each named field\n\nRepresentative uses (concrete tasks this pattern has shipped on):\n  - multi-source briefing: extracts from N articles → single executive summary with citations\n  - comparative report: per-vendor extractions → structured comparison + recommendation\n  - investigation conclusion: per-witness extractions → integrated timeline + assessment\n\n   Adopt / adapt / SPECIALIZE / mint-adjacent — use the evidence above, not the score alone:\n   - ADOPT this pattern as-is if it is an EXACT fit for your task.\n   - ADAPT it (keep the shape, override the specifics) if it mostly fits.\n   - SPECIALIZE — if this is a BROAD fit rather than an exact one (its `:summary`/strengths describe a more general shape than your task, or its `:avoid-when` flags your context), mint a domain-specialized CHILD of THIS behavior. The child keeps the parent's proven shape but pins your domain, gets a STABLE derived id, and ACCRUES evidence under the parent (it is retrievable on subsequent classify-behaviors calls):\n\n       (mint-behavior! \"<short-kebab-name>\"\n                       {:capabilities [\"<concrete action verb + object>\"]\n                        :strengths [{:trait \"<what works AND why>\"\n                                     :good-when \"<context guard>\"\n                                     :recommended-pattern \"<concrete DSL snippet>\"\n                                     :confidence 0.7\n                                     :evidence-count 1}]\n                        :weaknesses [{:trait \"<what fails AND why>\"\n                                      :avoid-when \"<context guard>\"\n                                      :recommended-alternative \"<concrete fix>\"\n                                      :confidence 0.7\n                                      :evidence-count 1}]\n                        :representative-uses [\"<concrete task this child shipped on>\"]\n                        :avoid-when [\"<concrete anti-context>\"]\n                        :summary \"<2-3 sentences naming the domain + load-bearing trait + when to prefer this over the parent>\"\n                        :version 1\n                        :consolidated-from-event-count 0}\n                       :parent 2bce84a1-d186-3892-8131-c19b59e4543e)\n\n   - MINT-ADJACENT — if your task is related-but-DISTINCT (not a child of this behavior), mint an adjacent behavior the same way with `:parent nil` (or under whichever listed reference is the true nearest parent).\n   :strengths/:weaknesses are VECTORS OF MAPS (principle-shaped: :trait + context-guard + concrete recommended action + :confidence + :evidence-count) — a vector of bare strings fails schema validation; the rejection is returned in iteration history for correction.\n\n2. Behavioral: Analysis (confidence: 0.85)\n   Why this fits: The task requires reasoning over extracted context (12 engineers, 3M tickets, 6-week timeline) to produce structured findings (tradeoffs in cost, quality, and fit). The 'Analysis' subtree's strength in 'multi-dimensional analysis' (using :parallel for risk/opportunity) is highly applicable to the multi-faceted comparison requested (quality vs. operational cost vs. team fit).\n\n   Guidance (seed `:summary`):\n   Analysis reasons over already-extracted items to produce structured findings (patterns / problems / opportunities) with per-finding evidence. Always tie findings to specific items via :affected-items references for downstream auditability. For multi-dimensional analysis, fan out per-dimension :llm stages via :parallel — each stage gets a tighter focus. NEVER skip the extraction stage: analyzing raw documents conflates extraction and reasoning, degrading both. Composes into comparative-summary (multi-source structured findings), chunked-extraction (per-chunk parallel analysis), and sequential-pipeline (extract → analyze → synthesize). Avoid when the task is summarizing (use synthesis) or generating options (use ideation).\n\nCapabilities:\n  - given a vector/map of pre-extracted items, produce a structured set of findings (patterns / problems / opportunities) with per-finding evidence\n  - natural fit as a mid-tree stage between extraction (upstream) and synthesis/critique/code-building (downstream)\n  - outputs are typically a vector of findings, each with :type / :evidence / :confidence / :affected-items\n\nStrengths (proven traits — these patterns have been observed to work; mimic where they fit, adapt as needed):\n  - **Trait:** tie each finding to the specific extracted item(s) that triggered it via an :affected-items reference field — downstream stages can audit, drill down, or prioritize findings by evidence weight (confidence 1.00, evidence-count 1)\n    - Good when: downstream stages need to attribute findings to evidence OR rank findings by support\n    - Worked example DSL (corpus reference — adapt to your task):\n      ```clojure\n      [:llm {:reads [:extracts] :writes [:findings] :output-schemas {:findings [:vector [:map [:type :string] [:evidence :string] [:confidence :double] [:affected-items [:vector :string]]]]}}]\n      ```\n\n  - **Trait:** for multi-dimensional analysis